 ## Operating characteristics
-*   **Time Complexity:** Inference is $O(K \cdot N)$ where $K=20$ is the number of stochastic passes and $N$ is the number of samples per ray, due to MC Dropout [S1, unofficial].
-*   **Memory Complexity:** Requires storing two parallel MLPs (standard and dual-head) and intermediate dropout masks during training [S1, unofficial].
-*   **Strengths:** Provides decomposed uncertainty (aleatoric and epistemic) and provable coverage guarantees via conformal prediction; robust to sparse, unstructured input [S1, unofficial].
-*   **Limitations:** Increased inference time due to multiple stochastic passes; requires careful calibration set construction [S1, unofficial].
-*   **When to Use:** Sparse view reconstruction of plants or similar structured natural scenes where uncertainty quantification is critical [S1, unofficial].
-*   **When Not to Use:** Dense view scenarios where standard NeRF suffices; real-time applications where $K=20$ passes are prohibitive [S1, unofficial].
+*   **Time Complexity:** Training is comparable to standard NeRF but slightly higher due to the dual-head and semantic loss. Inference requires $K=20$ forward passes for epistemic uncertainty, increasing inference time by a factor of 20 compared to deterministic NeRF [S1, unofficial].
+*   **Memory Complexity:** Requires storing parameters for two MLPs and the variance head. No additional large data structures beyond standard NeRF grids/volumes [S1, unofficial].
+*   **Strengths:** Provides calibrated uncertainty estimates, allowing users to distinguish reliable geometry from artifacts in sparse view scenarios. Effective for plants with occluded leaves [S1, unofficial].
+*   **Limitations:** High inference latency due to MC Dropout sampling. Performance depends on the quality of the initial frame selection (VISAR) [S1, unofficial].
+*   **When to use:** Sparse view reconstruction of plants where reliability assessment is critical.
+*   **When not to use:** Real-time applications requiring fast inference without uncertainty quantification.
 
 ## Background
-UA-PlantNeRF was introduced to address the limitations of standard NeRF in plant phenotyping, specifically degraded geometry under sparse, unstructured image capture and the lack of uncertainty estimates [S1, unofficial]. It builds on the VISAR frame-selection strategy and integrates concepts from heteroscedastic regression and conformal prediction [S1, unofficial]. Evaluated on benchmarks Pheno4D, ROSE-X, and the novel UA-Field greenhouse dataset [S1, unofficial].
+UA-PlantNeRF was introduced to address the specific challenges of 3D plant reconstruction from sparse, unstructured video, where traditional NeRFs suffer from geometry degradation and lack uncertainty metrics [S1, unofficial]. It builds upon the VISAR frame selection strategy and integrates concepts from heteroscedastic regression and conformal prediction [S1, unofficial].
 
